One common way improper use of Java APIs leads to vulnerabilities is through **untrusted deserialization**. When an application uses `ObjectInputStream.readObject()` on data provided by a user without validation, an attacker can supply a specially crafted serialized object that executes arbitrary code during the deserialization process.

```java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.ServerSocket;
import java.net.Socket;

public class VulnerableServer {
    public void startServer(int port) throws IOException {
        ServerSocket serverSocket = new ServerSocket(port);
        while (true) {
            try (Socket socket = serverSocket.accept();
                 ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(socket.getInputStream())) {

                // VULNERABLE: Deserializing untrusted data directly from the network
                // allows an attacker to perform Remote Code Execution (RCE) via gadget chains.
                Object obj = ois.readObject();
                System.out.println("Received object: " + obj.getClass().getName());
            } catch (Exception e) {
                e.printStackTrace();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

